[Music]  after decades of restoration efforts  wolves are once again roaming parts of  the western US the return of the animals  is not without controversy and a  remarkable new book examines the issues  involved from a unique perspective  through the life story of one of the  animals American wolf a true story of  survival and obsession in the West is by  author Nate Blakesley Nate good morning  and welcome good morning thanks for  having me so let's talk about the  reintroduction of the wolves to  Yellowstone National Park in 1995 how  did that happen and how successful was  it wolves were eliminated from  Yellowstone as they were from most of  the lower 48 by the end of the 19th  century and what happened in Yellowstone  was you saw this huge explosion in the  elk population that is the wolf's main  prey and there was nothing left to prey  on them and so in turn the habitat in  Yellowstone started to degrade  opportunities for other species were  lost because the elk you've probably  heard the expression the mountain fears  the elk like the elk here's the wolf the  idea that bringing wolves back might  solve this problem was first came up in  the 1940s but it was controversial and  the federal government wasn't able to  get it done until the mid 90s when they  did bring them back it was immensely  successful so much so that today the  debate is over whether or not they are  well established enough to begin hunting  them again Wow  the hero of this book is a wolf named oh  six what mate oh six so special oh six  his story captivated the world during  the time that that she was sort of the  queen of Yellowstone she was the most  visible wolf in the park at a time when  wolf watching became very popular and  Facebook was also exploding so her image  was everywhere I met this small group of  wolf watching fanatics in the park  people who come to the park every day  and track wolves and follow them with  their scopes and in particular I met  this one woman Laurie Lyman a retired  San Diego schoolteacher she gave me this  treasure trove of notes that she had  collected about oh six and her pack over  three years I read it it was like  reading the diary of a wolf pack and the  author in me said what you could do with  this material you could write a  nonfiction book that reads like a novel  in which the characters are wolves like  a Jack London store  except it's all true and well I don't  want to give any spoilers away but you  know the reintroduction of wolves is not  without controversy there are some  environmentalists on one side business  owners livestock owners who don't like  the reintroduction of wolves tell us  about the tension there  yes the same ranchers that eliminated  wolves generations ago their descendants  are still there and they still fear  losing cattle and sheep to wolves and  elk hunting is really big business in  the Northern Rockies and wolves eat a  lot of elk so there was a concern there  and also the politics around wolves has  become really poisonous it's become this  sort of us versus them this federal  government versus local control the  cultural issue isn't it in some ways it  is yeah the governor of Idaho butch  Otter he famously ran on a campaign of  promising to eliminate as many wolves  from Idaho as he could just as soon as  they came off the endangered species but  at the same time wolves have actually  kind of balanced the environment there  haven't been yes one of the goals as I  said of bringing wolves back 20 years  ago was to restore Yellowstone to fix  Yellowstone and now we've seen it this  biologists call it a trophic cascade you  you bring back the apex predator all of  a sudden there's fewer elk which in turn  means the habitat rebounds it's meant  healthier streams more fish more beavers  one of the first things that happened is  the coyote population plummeted because  wolves preyed on coyotes it brought  rodents back which in turn brought  Eagles back and it's just been this  endless cascade of positive effects a  fascinating neat Blakesley thank you so  much for being with us thank you I  appreciate it  the book is American wolf

